\section{Conclusion}

This thesis started from a question that road authorities face routinely and
answer informally: \emph{when a road segment must be closed, which of the possible
detours should be signposted?} The usual answer relies on local expertise and on
a visual reading of the network. The objective here was not to replace that
expertise, but to give it a reproducible experimental basis, a workflow in
which candidate deviation plans are generated systematically, simulated under
identical conditions, compared with paired statistics and ranked according to
criteria that are stated explicitly rather than left implicit.

\subsection*{What was built}

The result is an end-to-end pipeline built around SUMO. A scenario generator
synthesises network-aware demand from a road network and a Traffic Analysis Zone
layer, combining a gravity origin--destination model, a configurable temporal
profile and a $k$-shortest route library into routable trip definitions. A
deviation plan maker operates on a junction-level graph representation of the
network and produces candidate detours by three strategies: manual
specification, Yen's $k$-shortest loopless paths between the closure endpoints,
and an upstream search that moves the interception point backwards through the
network before searching, so that traffic can be diverted earlier than the
closed edge itself. A route rewriter applies each plan to the vehicles whose
routes traverse the closure. A Monte Carlo layer then executes repeated
simulations in which randomised edge weights perturb route assignment, pairing
each plan run with a baseline run generated under the same seed so that the
reported deltas measure the effect of the plan rather than an uncontrolled
difference between simulations. Finally, an evaluator rejects plans failing a
throughput feasibility gate, screens the survivors for Pareto dominance across
incremental delay, mean added travel time and the fraction of impacted users delayed
and ranks the non-dominated set with additive weighted scoring whose sensitivity
to the chosen weights is quantified by resampling the weight simplex.

\subsection*{What was established}

Four findings follow from the experiments.

First, automated candidate generation adds information that manual selection
misses. On the \emph{city\_tiny} network, Yen's algorithm returned replacement
paths shorter than either manually defined corridor while starting from the same
closure endpoints and the upstream search returned seventeen distinct
interception points spread over four levels of upstream depth, against the single
origin junction available to the manual and Yen plans. Those earlier interception
points are simply not visible when detours are defined only from the source
junction of the closed edge. Both automated families also achieved lower mean
incremental delay than the best manual plan in these runs, although the plan
families were not simulated with the same number of Monte Carlo replications, so
that particular comparison is indicative rather than established.

Second, the pipeline is not tied to the regular geometry of a generated test
network. Repeating the protocol on an OpenStreetMap extract around Flagey and
then on the multi-edge Delta tunnel closure in Auderghem, produced routable
demand, valid plan representations, reproducible Monte Carlo variation and an
interpretable recommendation in every case. The Delta case also demonstrates
that the workflow extends from an illustrative single-edge closure to a
scheduled, time-limited closure of the kind that motivates the tool in practice.

Third, the decision problem is genuinely multi-criteria and treating it as
single-objective would have hidden that. In both real topologies the plan
minimising aggregate delay was not the plan distributing the burden most evenly
across the impacted population and the recommended plan ranked first in only
about three quarters of sampled weight vectors. At Delta the short bypass
inconveniences a marginally larger share of tunnel users while costing each of
them far less time (180 against roughly 700 vehicle-minutes over the
impacted population). Exposing that tension is more useful to a decision maker
than resolving it arbitrarily.

Fourth and most strongly, the exposure regime changes the stakes of the
decision even when it does not change the decision. Re-running the identical
Delta network, plans and demand over a full day instead of the $23{:}00$--$06{:}00$
window increased the exposed demand by a factor of $13.1$, but the delay penalty
of the two routes grew by factors of $48.8$ and $52.1$ while the short
bypass grew by $13.6$, essentially in proportion to demand. The recommendation
was unchanged, yet the cost of choosing wrongly rose from about $2.3$ to about
$175$~vehicle-hours and the worst-to-best ratio widened from $2.6$ to $9.6$, one plan additionally lost its feasibility. A plan that looks merely
suboptimal under light demand can be an operational failure under full demand,
which argues for evaluating candidates at the loading they will actually
experience rather than at a convenient one.

\subsection*{What was not established}

The framework's correctness was demonstrated, not its predictive accuracy. All
demand is synthetic: origin--destination flows come from a gravity model with
assumed parameters, zoning is an automatic grid rather than a calibrated
partition and the external-traffic share and daily trip totals are
configuration constants rather than observed quantities. No result presented
here should be read as a forecast of delay for Flagey or Delta. The pipeline
models a single vehicle class, excludes intra-zonal trips, resolves demand at
hourly granularity, holds the vehicle population fixed across Monte Carlo runs
so that only route-assignment uncertainty is captured and generates plans
statically from free-flow topology at the moment the closure is declared. These
choices are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:limitations} and bound the external
validity of every number reported.

\subsection*{Perspectives}

Three extensions would be the most valuable to prioritise. The first is
validation against an observed closure: comparing simulated detour flows with
counting-device measurements collected during a real roadworks event is the only
way to test whether the ranking the framework produces matches what happens on
the road. The second is demand-side uncertainty. Because stochasticity currently
enters only through randomised route assignment, the reported confidence
intervals understate the true uncertainty of a closure's impact, resampling
departure times, trip cancellations and information-diffusion delays across
Monte Carlo runs would reflect the decision risk more honestly. The third is
relaxing the single-closure assumption, so that contiguous worksites and
concurrent closures can be planned jointly rather than in isolation, since plans
that are optimal separately may conflict when applied together. Beyond these,
feeding live traffic states into plan generation and adding equity and
spatial-distribution criteria to the evaluator would move the framework closer
to the operational role it is designed to serve.

Even in its present form, however, it makes the case for a modest but concrete
shift in practice: deviation plans are comparable objects and a city that
generates them systematically, simulates them under identical conditions and
documents why one was preferred has a defensible answer where it previously had
only an informed opinion.
